(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-14"] = {"7.1":{"s":"A random variable turns each outcome of a chance experiment into a real number: a rule defined on the sample space, so its value is not known until the experiment is done. For example, the sum of two dice or the number of heads in some tosses. Studying the probabilities of its values (its distribution) lets us describe chance experiments with numbers.","w":["A random variable is a function on the sample space; one outcome gives exactly one value."]},"7.2":{"s":"A discrete random variable takes only a finite or countable list of values (the number of heads, the sum on dice). A continuous random variable takes any value in an interval of real numbers, found by measurement (a time or a length). The two kinds have different kinds of probability distribution.","w":["Counting gives a discrete variable; measuring gives a continuous one."]},"7.3":{"s":"The probability distribution of a discrete random variable lists its values x with their probabilities p; each p is at least 0 and the probabilities add to 1. This is the probability mass function, and the cumulative distribution function F(x) adds the probabilities up to x. The expected value (mean) is the sum of each value times its probability, and the variance measures the spread around it; the standard deviation is its square root.","f":[{"x":"p_i = P[X = x_i] >= 0 and Σp_i = 1","n":"Conditions for a probability distribution (p.m.f.).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.223"},{"x":"F(x) = P[X <= x] = Σp_i over all x_i <= x","n":"Cumulative distribution function of a discrete variable.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.224"},{"x":"E(X) = Σx_i*p_i","n":"Expected value (mean) of X.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.229"},{"x":"Var(X) = Σ(x_i - mu)^2*p_i","n":"Variance; mu = E(X). The standard deviation is the square root of Var(X).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.229"},{"x":"Var(X) = E(X^2) - (E(X))^2","n":"Simplified form; E(X^2) = Σx_i^2*p_i.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.3, p.229"}],"w":["If your probabilities do not add up to 1, find the unknown constant first.","Var(X) = E(X²) − [E(X)]²: square the mean AFTER averaging, not before."]},"7.4":{"s":"For a continuous random variable the probability of any single value is zero, so probabilities come from areas: a probability density function f is never negative, the total area under it is 1, and the probability of an interval is the integral of f over it. The cumulative distribution function F(x) is the integral of f from the lower end of the range up to x, and it never decreases.","f":[{"x":"f(x) >= 0 and ∫_S f(x) dx = 1","n":"Conditions for a probability density function on the support S.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.4, p.234"},{"x":"P[X in A] = ∫_A f(x) dx","n":"Probability that X lies in the interval A.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.4, p.234"},{"x":"F(x) = ∫_a^x f(t) dt","n":"Cumulative distribution function for a < x < b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, 7.4, p.234"}],"w":["f(x) is a height, not a probability: it can exceed 1, only areas are probabilities."]}};
